  "explain": "<p>About twice, varying from run to run between 1.7 and 2.5 in e14. With oversampling 10 and two power iterations it is within half a percent. The Frobenius error looks much better, about 20 percent above optimal, because the long tail it sums is large for every method; the spectral norm shows the largest direction the method missed.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "M33/concept1",
  "lesson": "M33",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why is a large matrix product compute-bound while a matrix-vector product is memory-bound, on the same chip?",
  "answer": "A matrix product of n by n matrices does 2 n^3 flops on 3 n^2 numbers, so its arithmetic intensity grows like n and exceeds the chip's ridge point, the ratio of peak flops to memory bandwidth. A matrix-vector product does 2 flops per matrix entry it reads, about half a flop per byte in fp32 at any size, far below the ridge point, so it runs at the speed memory can deliver data.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
